   "citationRecall": 0.5,
   "text": "The refusal gate is a retrieval-side floor that can refuse a question before the model is called. If a question has no support, it costs zero model calls and produces no generated text. The gate's behavior is controlled by `guard.mode`, which defaults to `'off'` since version 1.3. In this mode, the gate is always scored and recorded, but the model decides whether to refuse based on a threshold calibrated per corpus and language. Setting `guard.mode: 'calibrated'` turns the gate into a decision-maker for a single-language corpus with a calibrated probe set.\n\nThe refusal gate can issue a verdict of `no-answer`, which has five causes. Three of these are the gate's decisions, while the other two are resolved before the gate runs. These include `credential` (when the question contains a live key), `social` (when the input is a greeting), `no-evidence` (when the question is below threshold and widening the scope won't help), `out-of-scope` (when the question is below threshold but spans the entire corpus), and `not-answerable` (when the model returns nothing citable)."
  },
